+/* Guard automatic fire: the same tick-modulo defect the player had, in the AI.
+ *
+ * `firecount[hand]` advances once per TICK and the gate is `firecount % rate == 0`, so under
+ * GETV_SIMDIV guards fire once every N ticks whatever a tick is worth in real time. Measured
+ * on Bunker 1, identical scripted run, n=3 per divider and byte-identical within each:
+ *
+ *     SIMDIV=1  Bond ends on hp 0.69      SIMDIV=2  Bond ends on hp 0.81
+ *
+ * Guards were dealing about 1.6x the damage per real second at divider 1. That is the AI
+ * half of what gunfire.c:3855 was for the player.
+ *
+ * firecount is kept as the counter and its UNITS change from ticks to fields: it advances by
+ * g_ClockTimer, and the gate asks whether it crossed a multiple of the rate rather than
+ * landing exactly on one, because a counter stepping by n can step straight over the equality.
+ *
+ * At divider 1 g_ClockTimer is 1, the counter advances by 1, and the crossing test is exactly
+ * the modulo it replaces -- (cur-1)/rate differs from cur/rate precisely when cur is a
+ * multiple of rate. Verified by measurement, not by argument.
+ *
+ * Shares GETV_TIMEFIRE with the player-side conversion: they are one behaviour and must not
+ * be separately switchable, or an A/B would compare a mixed state. */
+/* GETV_CHR_FIRERATE=<item>: give every guard another weapon's FIRING CADENCE.
+ *
+ * Two uses, both real. It is the only way to make guard fire the dominant term in a timing
+ * measurement -- with issued weapons the signal is weak and slow, and a scripted run can sit
+ * for 1400 frames before anything shoots back. And it is a stress test: a room of RC-P90s
+ * puts far more beams, sounds, impacts and animation through the frame than any authored
+ * encounter, which is exactly the load that exposes frame-timing and rendering problems.
+ *
+ * 🔴 It does NOT change the weapon a guard carries. It redirects the four
+ * bondwalkItemGetAutomaticFiringRate() reads in the firing block and nothing else, so guards
+ * shoot at the chosen weapon's rhythm while still holding, animating and sounding their own
+ * gun. Named for what it does: an earlier name implied a weapon swap and that was wrong.
+ *
+ * A real swap has to replace chr->weapons_held[], which is a prop pointer to a weapon OBJECT
+ * spawned from the setup file (propobj.c:12111 only attaches what already exists), so it needs
+ * the object and model pipeline. That is the horde-difficulty feature and it is scoped in
+ * docs/STANCE.md rather than half-built here.
+ *
+ * Off unless set; the value is an ITEM_ id -- 10 is the D5K, 14 the FN P90, 25 the rocket
+ * launcher.
+ *
+ * ⚠️ ITEM_LASER, ITEM_ROCKETLAUNCH and ITEM_GRENADELAUNCH take different code paths in the
+ * firing block; overriding to one of those is legitimate but exercises a different branch. */
